          {
            "id": "p10-q9-short-reason",
            "type": "paragraph",
            "fontSize": null,
            "align": "left",
            "spaceBefore": 0,
            "spaceAfter": 2,
            "lineHeight": 1.4,
            "indent": 0,
            "inlines": [
              {
                "type": "text",
                "text": "Excluding the origin removes the intercept; values still approach zero near it.",
                "marks": []
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      "worked": "Define $f(x)=x$ with domain $\\mathbb{R}\\setminus\\{0\\}$. The point $(0,0)$ is excluded, so $f$ has no $x$-intercept.\n$$\\frac{1}{f(x)}=\\frac{1}{x},\\qquad x\\ne0.$$\nAs $x$ approaches $0$ from the right, $\\frac{1}{x}$ approaches $+\\infty$; from the left, it approaches $-\\infty$. Therefore the reciprocal graph has vertical asymptote $x=0$.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
